  "context_pill": "Agricultural Income & Exemptions",
  "example_summary": "This case study examines the taxability of rent from agricultural buildings used for residential purposes, nursery income without basic land operations, and the specific regional exemptions available to Sikkimese individuals.",
  "source_provision": "| Statutory Area | Section / Rule | Core Statutory Condition / Test | Tax Treatment & Legal Consequence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **General Agricultural Income** | Section 10(1) r/w Sec 2(1A) | Revenue derived from land situated in India and used for agricultural operations. | **Exempt** from total income. |\n| **Building on Agricultural Land** | Explanation 2 to Section 2(1A) | Income from buildings used for non-agricultural purposes (e.g. residential/industrial letting). | **Taxable** as non-agricultural income. |\n| **Nursery Operations** | Explanation 3 to Section 2(1A) | Income from saplings or seedlings grown in a nursery, irrespective of basic land operations. | **Exempt** as deemed agricultural income. |\n| **Sikkimese Regional Exemption** | Section 10(26AA) | Income accruing to a Sikkimese individual from any source in Sikkim or via interest/dividends. | **Exempt** from income tax. |\n\n\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Statutory Trap on Agricultural Buildings: Letting out houses constructed on agricultural land to industrial workers for residential purposes fails the test under Explanation 2 to Section 2(1A) and is fully chargeable to tax.\n\n\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Nursery Exception: By virtue of Explanation 3 to Section 2(1A), carrying out basic operations on land is not mandatory for income derived from saplings or seedlings grown in a nursery.",
